Below is a quick‑reference guide you can drop straight into your project.  
It shows **how** to use the supplied helper (`prioritizeAndSort`) so that:

1. All *non‑urgent* items are sorted alphabetically by `name` – ascending.  
2. Items whose `status === “urgent”` always appear first (in whatever order they originally were).

---

### 1️⃣ Install / import the helper

```js
// If it lives in its own module:
import { prioritizeAndSort } from './utils/prioritizer';
```

> In case you’re running this directly in Node/Browser without modules, copy the implementation below instead of importing.

---

### 2️⃣ Example Implementation (if you don’t already have one)

```js
/**
 * Sorts an array of objects by `name`, putting all items whose
 * `status` matches `priorityStatus` before everything else.
 *
 * @param {{name:string,status:string}[]} items          - Array to sort
 * @param {string}       priorityStatus                 - Status that gets priority (“urgent”, etc.)
 * @param {boolean}      ascending                      - true → alphabetical ASC, false → DESC
 * @returns {Array<{name:string,status:string}>}
 */
export const prioritizeAndSort = ({ items, priorityStatus, ascending }) => {
    const [priorityItems, otherItems] = items.reduce(
        ([pri, oth], item) =>
            item.status === priorityStatus ? [[...pri, item], oth]
                                            : [pri, [...oth, item]],
        [[], []]
    );

    const compareFn = ascending
        ? (a,b)=> a.name.localeCompare(b.name)
        : (a,b)=> b.name.localeCompare(a.name);

    return [...priorityItems.sort(compareFn), ...otherItems.sort(compareFn)];
};
```

*(If you prefer inline code, skip step 3.)*

---

### 3️⃣ Using the Helper With Your Data

Assume you have:

```js
const myItemList = [
    { name: 'Zebra',   status: 'normal' },
    { name: 'Apple',   status: 'urgent' },
    { name: 'Mango',   status: 'normal' },
    { name: 'Banana',  status: 'urgent' }
];
```

Call the sorter like this:

```js
const sorted = prioritizeAndSort({
    items: myItemList,
    priorityStatus: 'urgent',
    ascending: true     // change to false for reverse alphabetical
});

console.log(sorted);
/*
[
  { name: 'Apple',  status: 'urgent' },   // urgent items stay together
  { name: 'Banana', status: 'urgent' },

  { name: 'Mango',  status: 'normal' },   // rest sorted alphabetically
  { name: 'Zebra',  status: 'normal' }
]
*/
```

#### What Happens Internally?

| Step | Action |
|------|--------|
| 1 | Split the original array into two buckets:<br>`priorityItems` (where `status==='urgent')`<br>`otherItems` (everything else). |
| 2 | Alphabetically sort each bucket separately (`localeCompare`). |
| 3 | Concatenate `[sortedPriority, sortedOthers]`. |

Because the split happens **before** sorting, all urgent items automatically precede non‑urgent ones regardless of their names.

---

### 4️⃣ Quick One‑liner Alternative (no external helper)

If you’d rather avoid pulling in another file, here’s a concise version you can paste right where you need it:

```js
function prioritizeAndSort(items, priorityStatus='urgent', ascending=true){
    const cmp = ascending ?
                ((a,b)=>a.name.localeCompare(b.name)) :
                ((a,b)=>b.name.localeCompare(a.name));
    const pri = [], others=[];
    for(const i of items){(i.status===priorityStatus?pri:others).push(i);}
    return [...pri.sort(cmp), ...others.sort(cmp)];
}

// Usage same as above…
```

Feel free to tweak the default values (`priorityStatus`, `ascending`) to match your conventions.

Happy coding! 🚀
